    "output": "(1) \"socket2\\nsocket1\\nopened-ok\\n\": the stdio pipes from runProcess are sockets. macOS /dev/fd can still open them, but Linux /proc/<pid>/fd/N returns ENXIO for a socket. (2) the error points at test/setup.ts:8:3; \"0 pass 1 fail 1 error\", exit=1 after 48ms. When the preload's marker write fails, bun exits 1 immediately and never reaches the hang."
   },
   "evidence": "Head test lines 142-162 run `sh([\"bash\",\"-c\",'export LIMITLESS_CI_TEST_LOG=\"/proc/$$/fd/2\"; bash \"$@\"', ...])`. sh/runProcess spawns with `stdio: [\"pipe\",\"pipe\",\"pipe\"]` (src/util/proc.ts:533-542), which are socketpairs. setup.ts:7-9 calls `appendFileSync(process.env.LIMITLESS_CI_TEST_LOG, ...)` with no guard, so the throw makes every file error out. The monitor's `\"$@\" & wait \"$!\"; exit $?` passes bun's 1 through, and timeout returns 1. The wrapper itself is not at fault: in the real CI step, fd 2 is a pipe from the runner. That is why the plain-spawn wrapper tests pass and only this nested case gets 1. The reviewer's guesses about set -m or job control are not the cause."
